{"name":"Great Graphite","hex":"#a5a6a1","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"great","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Taking up a lot of space; large.\n\n Much, more than usual.","Much, more than usual."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Expression of gladness and content about something.","A sarcastic inversion thereof."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/great"},{"term":"graphite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An allotrope of carbon, consisting of planes of carbon atoms arranged in hexagonal arrays with the planes stacked loosely, that is used as a dry lubricant, in \"lead\" pencils, and as a moderator in some nuclear reactors.","Graphite-reinforced plastic, a composite plastic made with graphite fibers noted for lightweight strength and stiffness."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To apply graphite to."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/graphite"}],"entries":[{"title":"Graphite","blocks":[{"text":"Graphite is a crystalline allotrope (form) of the element carbon. It consists of many stacked layers of graphene, typically in excess of hundreds of layers. Graphite occurs naturally and is the most stable form of carbon under standard conditions. Synthetic and natural graphite are consumed on a large scale for uses in many critical industries including refractories (50%), lithium-ion batteries (18%), foundries (10%), and lubricants (5%), among others (17%). Graphite converts to diamond under extremely high pressure and temperature. Graphite's low cost, thermal and chemical inertness and characteristic conductivity of heat and electricity finds numerous applications in high energy and high temperature processes."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Graphite-233436.jpg/330px-Graphite-233436.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graphite","wikidata":"Q5309"}],"images":[{"srcset":